# FOS adapter entry-point notes

The entry point is in `src/Elastica/Client.php`. Don't conflate it with the index template or population paths: what we can see here is the client-layer dependency surface for adapting to FOS.

The aliases at the top of the file are worth retaining:

```php
use Elastica\Client as BaseClient;
use Elastica\Exception\ClientException;
use Elastica\Index as BaseIndex;
use Elastica\Request;
```

`BaseClient` and `BaseIndex` both use aliases rather than their original names. Refer back to these two aliases when you reach the client or index objects later in the file. `Request` keeps its original name and is the only request object directly visible in this file; `ClientException` is an exception type already declared at the same entry point.

This pass only confirmed the immediate relationships at the top of the file. I haven't yet noted which method assembles the actual request or which branch throws the exception. If we later need to fill in the request compatibility behavior for FOS, first trace back from the uses of `Request`, then check whether the base class behind the alias already handles that behavior. The history of the compatibility window itself is in DOC-10.
